      "content": "Given the complexities of the issue, I lean towards arguing that the expansion of executive privilege can be a necessary protection for the presidency and checks on congressional oversight. You can frame your argument by citing recent cases such as Nixon v. United States (1974) and his subsequent executive privilege, extending congressional deference to the president's pre-existing privilege as a long-standing principle of executive immunity. This can also include analyzing how extreme court decisions may result in unpredictable and uncontrollable duplication of executive authority, effectively subverting the constitutional structure of government-established checks and balances.",
